sitional kernels; row index\\n  1914\\t             on the walker; closures carry :store-of; the\\n  1915\\t             register frame drops :segment and :hash; store\\n  1916\\t             routing and its frame threading)                      (M4)\\n  1917\\t          src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc  (append-*-image over attach-image;\\n  1918\\t                                   the four streams)          (M4, M5)\\n  1919\\t          docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md\\n  1920\\t            (section 7.4.1 rows, 7.4.3 variants, 7.5.1 marker\\n  1921\\t             keys :yin.k/binding and :yin.k/store-of)              (M4)\\n  1922\\tDepends:  dao.jing (segment-key, segment-matches?, materialize!)\\n  1923\\t          dao.jing.remote, dao.jing.remote.step, dao.stream.rpc\\n  1924\\t          yin.vm (validate-rows, free-names, primitive-profiles)\\n  1925\\t          yin.vm.code (well-formed-vector?)\\n  1926\\t          yin.vm.ledger (derive-record, verify-derivation)\\n  1927\\t          yin.vm.debruijn-code, yin.vm.debruijn-register-code\\n  1928\\tMust not change:\\n  1929\\t          image-hash, register-hash, segment-key, the opcode\\n  1930\\t          tables, dao.stream, dao.jing. No kernel's step function\\n  1931\\t          changes its decode, dispatch, or register model; the\\n  1932\\t          behavioral amendments r6 and r7 make at a kernel are\\n  1933\\t          closure-directed resolution and store routing, the\\n  1934\\t          :store-of consult and its frame threading (7.3).\\n  1935\\t```\\n  1936\\t\\n  1937\\t`yin.vm.linker` exports: `fetch`, `verify`, `discharge`, `link-state`,\\n  1938\\t`request-link`, `step`, `abandon`, `publish!`, `ast-format`,\\n  1939\\t`semantic-format`, `stack-format`, `register-format`, `manifest-format`,\\n  1940\\t`record-format`, the four free-name, definition, and application-site\\n  1941\\tscanners, `row-local-defect`, `index-from-datoms`, `address-attribute`,\\n  1942\\t`trusted-fallback`, `verifying-fallback`, `refused`, `refused?`, `ok?`,\\n  1943\\t`refusal-reasons`.\\n  1944\\t\\n  2050\\t    `:descriptor-defect` with rule `:schema`; a derivation without a\\n  2051\\t    contract entry is `:descriptor-defect` with rule\\n  2052\\t    `:contract-missing`.\\n  2053\\t23. On every backend, a module that binds `(def x 1)` and exports a\\n  2054\\t    closure reading `x` links, and the closure applied in the parent\\n  2055\\t    reads the module's `x`, while a parent binding of the same name\\n  2056\\t    neither supplies nor shadows it; a write the closure makes to\\n  2057\\t    `x` through a direct `:store-put` instruction is visible to a\\n  2058\\t    sibling export applied afterwards in the same task and to no\\n  2059\\t    other task's module store; the same module linked by two tasks\\n  2060\\t    holds two isolated stores; a slice whose exports reach a\\n  2061\\t    dependency's store transitively carries that dependency's\\n  2062\\t    snapshot, including a mutation the child made through the\\n  2063\\t    dependency's closure, a missing snapshot is\\n  2064\\t    `:yin.k/non-portable` with `:yin.k/kind :missing-module-store`,\\n  2065\\t    and a receiving task that already holds the dependency's store\\n  2066\\t    keeps its own instance; a module closure calling another\\n  2067\\t    module's closure routes each body's reads and writes to its own\\n  2068\\t    module; in the child, a closure re-exported from a dependency\\n  2069\\t    reads that dependency's module store, not the child's ambient\\n  2070\\t    bindings; a module emitting `[:store-get :stream-0]` against a\\n  2071\\t    task with a live stream at that id reads nothing on any\\n  2072\\t    backend, because engine resources live in the private table no\\n  2073\\t    store instruction reads; after a module closure applied at the\\n  2074\\t    REPL and its tail call completed, new top-level input defining\\n  2075\\t    a name of its own binds the task's store, never the module's;\\n  2076\\t    a walker closure whose body node sits under two `:lambda` rows\\n  2077\\t    with different params lifts from its recorded source row --\\n  2078\\t    annotated on the decoded node at load, copied into the closure\\n  2079\\t    at evaluation -- and a fabricated closure with no recorded row\\n  2080\\t    resolves by the `[node params]` index or refuses\\n  2081\\t    `:unrooted-body`.\\n  2082\\t\\n  2083\\t## 12. Open decisions\\n  2130\\t\\n  2131\\t### 13.1 Revision r8 (round 7, `gpt-6-sol`)\\n  2132\\t\\n  2133\\tThe round-7 review closed the grow-only-returns item (no action)\\n  2134\\tand left two partial findings. The round-7 findings file was not\\n  2135\\tpresent in `collab/`; r8 worked from the two items in the task.\\n  2136\\tEach, and what r8 did:\\n  2137\\t\\n  2138\\t| Item                              | Resolution                        |\\n  2139\\t|-----------------------------------|-----------------------------------|\\n  2140\\t| r7's `:store-get` fallback to the | Agreed; r7 rule withdrawn. Engine |\\n  2141\\t| ambient store was a forging       | resources (stream handles, cursor |\\n  2142\\t| vector: resource keys are         | cells, the FFI pair) move to a    |\\n  2143\\t| counter-minted and predictable,   | private `:resources` table no     |\\n  2144\\t| so a module could read a host     | store instruction or `resolve-var`|\\n  2145\\t| handle it was never handed; the   | step reads; store instructions    |\\n  2146\\t| active context could go stale     | have no ambient fallback at all;  |\\n  2147\\t| after a tail call                 | ids may stay predictable, they    |\\n  2148\\t|                                   | name nothing reachable (a UCF    |\\n  2149\\t|                                   | 4.1/7.6.2 amendment, criterion    |\\n  2150\\t|                                   | 12). A tail call that empties the |\\n  2151\\t|                                   | continuation clears the active    |\\n  2152\\t|                                   | context, and new top-level input  |\\n  2153\\t|                                   | is admitted only with none active |\\n  2154\\t|                                   | (7.3 private engine resources,    |\\n  2155\\t|                                   | run-time rule; criterion 23; M4   |\\n  2156\\t|                                   | forged-key and stale-context      |\\n  2157\\t|                                   | tests).                           |\\n  2158\\t| r7 conflated decode time with     | Agreed. Two stages: the row       |\\n  2159\\t| evaluation time for the walker's  | decoder annotates each decoded    |\\n  2160\\t| lambda row id; the closure is     | `:lambda` node with its source    |\\n  2161\\t| instantiated only when the node   | row id (or a side index; one node |\\n  2162\\t| is evaluated                      | per row id, so unambiguous), and  |\\n  2163\\t|                                   | the runtime `:lambda` transition  |\\n  2164\\t|                                   | copies the annotation into the    |\\n  2165\\t|                                   | closure it instantiates; the lift |\\n  2166\\t|                                   | verifies body slot and params as  |\\n  2167\\t|                                   | before (7.3 walker rule,          |\\n  2168\\t|                                   | criterion 23).                    |\\n  2169\\t\\n  2170\\t### 13.2 Revision r7 (round 6, `gpt-6-sol`)\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/1790256330629-reviewer-universal-linker-consensus-r8.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log:11:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_4\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | sed -n '1250,1308p;1310,1424p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"  1250\\t  |                                      | `mapv (mapv encode) F`,       |\\n  1251\\t  |                                      | outermost frame first         |\\n  1252\\t\\n  1253\\t  | Marker variant | Lower into the parent                              |\\n  1254\\t  |----------------|----------------------------------------------------|\\n  1255\\t  | `:named`,      | `local'` = parent alias column at `addr`; `:entry` |\\n  1256\\t  | semantic       | unchanged; `:params` unchanged; `:env` =           |\\n  1257\\t  |                | `decode(env)`                                      |\\n  1258\\t  | `:named`,      | `:body` = the body slot of the parent's row at     |\\n  1259\\t  | walker         | `addr`, reconstructed as a node by the walker's    |\\n  1260\\t  |                | row decoder; `:params` unchanged; `:env` =         |\\n  1261\\t  |                | `decode(env)`                                      |\\n  1262\\t  | `:positional`  | `off'` = parent offset-table entry for `identity`; |\\n  1263\\t  |                | `:body-pc` = `off' + pc`; `:arity` = `n`;          |\\n  1264\\t  |                | `:frames` = `mapv (mapv decode) frames`            |\\n  1265\\t\\n  1266\\t  **The walker rule, exactly.** A walker closure holds its body as a\\n  1267\\t  live AST node, so its code coordinate is not a field of the closure\\n  1268\\t  but a fact about the rows: the body is the `body` slot of a\\n  1269\\t  `:lambda` row in the rows the child holds, and that row has an id\\n  1270\\t  (its `segment-key`, `yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` section 4.1). The\\n  1271\\t  row is **not** found by the node alone: rows are content-addressed\\n  1272\\t  and shared, and `semantic-bytecode->ast` rebuilds \\\"a row reached\\n  1273\\t  through several parents ... once and shared\\\", so one body node can\\n  1274\\t  sit under several `:lambda` rows with different params, and a\\n  1275\\t  `node -> row id` index cannot select among them. The id therefore\\n  1276\\t  crosses in two stages, because two different machines build the\\n  1277\\t  two values: the row decoder builds AST nodes at load, while a\\n  1278\\t  runtime closure is instantiated only when the `:lambda` node is\\n  1279\\t  evaluated. At load, the decoder annotates each decoded `:lambda`\\n  1280\\t  node with its source row id -- or files it in a side index\\n  1281\\t  `lambda-node -> row id`; the rebuild memoizes one node per row\\n  1282\\t  id, so the mapping is unambiguous -- and at evaluation the\\n  1283\\t  runtime `:lambda` transition (`ast-walker`'s `case :lambda`)\\n  1284\\t  copies the annotation into the closure it instantiates. The lift\\n  1285\\t  reads the recorded id and verifies the row's body slot is the\\n  1286\\t  closure's node and its params are the closure's params; a\\n  1287\\t  disagreement is the same `:yin.k/non-portable` `:unrooted-body`\\n  1288\\t  refusal as an id in no held row. A closure with no recorded id,\\n  1289\\t  which only a host function can fabricate, resolves through the\\n  1290\\t  walker's row index keyed by the pair `[node params]` (the index\\n  1291\\t  `vm-load-rows` builds when it decodes rows into nodes and\\n  1292\\t  `attach-image` extends), and refuses when the pair matches no\\n  1293\\t  row. The origin image of a walker closure\\n  1294\\t  is the tree whose rows contain that `:lambda` row; the `:lambda`\\n  1295\\t  row id is content-addressed, so the same lambda shared by two\\n  1296\\t  trees is one id and either tree serves as origin. The lower reads\\n  1297\\t  the parent's row at that id (present after act 3) and decodes its\\n  1298\\t  body slot into a node through the same decoder the loader uses, so\\n  1299\\t  the closure's `:body` in the parent is structurally equal to the\\n  1300\\t  child's and its `:params` and decoded `:env` are carried unchanged.\\n  1301\\t\\n  1302\\t  A `:named` marker lowers only into a named kernel and a `:positional`\\n  1303\\t  marker only into a positional kernel of the same `:format`; the\\n  1304\\t  reverse is `:binding-mismatch`. This never arises within one install,\\n  1305\\t  because the child is a VM of the same backend as the parent (section\\n  1306\\t  7.3, first rule); it is stated so that a lifted slice carried\\n  1307\\t  elsewhere fails closed rather than being reinterpreted. Converting a\\n  1308\\t  closure between disciplines is a re-lowering of source under a\\n  1310\\t  `encode` recurse through frames and environments, so a captured\\n  1311\\t  closure is relocated by the same rules and its origin joins the\\n  1312\\t  origin set of act 2.\\n  1313\\t\\n  1314\\t  **The module store (r6, amended r7).** A child runs with an\\n  1315\\t  isolated store, and its exports must not leak that isolation. A\\n  1316\\t  module that binds `(def x 1)` and exports `(fn [] x)` publishes a\\n  1317\\t  closure whose free read of `x` would, if only the closure value\\n  1318\\t  crossed the boundary, resolve through the receiving task's store\\n  1319\\t  and fall through to its primitives and module registry -- where\\n  1320\\t  `x` is unbound or bound to something the publisher never saw. The\\n  1321\\t  stores therefore cross with the exports, under UCF section 7.6.2's\\n  1322\\t  slice discipline:\\n  1323\\t  - *Lift.* One snapshot is encoded per module, not just the child's\\n  1324\\t    own: the child's halted store under its manifest address, and\\n  1325\\t    for every dependency whose store the exports reach transitively\\n  1326\\t    (a re-exported closure carries that dependency's `:store-of`),\\n  1327\\t    that dependency's store instance as it stands in the child's\\n  1328\\t    state at halt -- including mutations the child made through the\\n  1329\\t    dependency's own closures, which a snapshot of the child's store\\n  1330\\t    alone would not carry. A `:store-of` with no snapshot is a lift\\n  1331\\t    refusal, `:yin.k/non-portable` with\\n  1332\\t    `:yin.k/kind :missing-module-store`. Each snapshot encodes as a\\n  1333\\t    `:yin.k/store` slice (UCF 7.6.2); whatever that grammar refuses\\n  1334\\t    (a host object in the store; engine resources cannot be, by the\\n  1335\\t    private-resource rule below) refuses the lift and the install.\\n  1336\\t  - *Marker.* Every closure marker names the store its body resolves\\n  1337\\t    against, `:yin.k/store-of m` with `m` the module's manifest\\n  1338\\t    address -- an amendment to UCF section 7.5.1 listed in section\\n  1339\\t    11, item 12. The snapshots travel once per slice, not per\\n  1340\\t    closure.\\n  1341\\t  - *Lower.* Each receiving task instantiates every snapshot into\\n  1342\\t    `:module-stores {m {sym value}}` in its own state, one instance\\n  1343\\t    per module: a store the task already holds stands as\\n  1344\\t    instantiated -- first link wins, and a later slice never\\n  1345\\t    overwrites a live instance -- while snapshots it lacks are\\n  1346\\t    instantiated fresh, so a task sees exactly one store per module.\\n  1347\\t    Nothing is written into any task's ambient store: this is\\n  1348\\t    7.6.2's \\\"there is no merge\\\" rule at the module grain -- a\\n  1349\\t    receiver binding of the same name can neither supply nor shadow\\n  1350\\t    the module's value.\\n  1351\\t  - *Run time.* While a closure carrying `:store-of m` executes, the\\n  1352\\t    module store of m is its **active store**: free reads resolve\\n  1353\\t    through it first and the receiver's free env, primitives, and\\n  1354\\t    registry second; the ambient store does not participate. Direct\\n  1355\\t    store instructions route by the same context -- `:store-put`\\n  1356\\t    (and the `:vm/store-put` effect) writes the active module\\n  1357\\t    store, and `:store-get` reads it with **no ambient fallback**:\\n  1358\\t    a key the module store lacks is absent, exactly as a top-level\\n  1359\\t    read of an absent key is; engine resources are unreachable\\n  1360\\t    either way, by the private-resource rule below. The context\\n  1361\\t    threads through calls: a frame records the caller's active\\n  1362\\t    `:store-of` (or none), applying a closure makes that closure's\\n  1363\\t    `:store-of` active, and returning restores the frame's, so a\\n  1364\\t    module closure calling another module's closure routes each\\n  1365\\t    body's reads and writes to its own module. A tail call that\\n  1366\\t    empties the continuation -- the closure's body was the whole\\n  1367\\t    computation -- clears the active context with the last frame,\\n  1368\\t    and the VM admits new top-level input only with no active\\n  1369\\t    context, which `yin.repl`'s append asserts, so input after a\\n  1370\\t    module closure applied never routes through a stale module\\n  1371\\t    store. Sibling exports in one task share one store instance,\\n  1372\\t    so a write one makes is visible to the next; each task holds\\n  1373\\t    its own instances, so writes never cross tasks. A declared\\n  1374\\t    obligation the store does not hold falls through to the\\n  1375\\t    receiver's bindings exactly as step 5b checked them; a\\n  1376\\t    conditional definition that ran overwrites the module's own\\n  1377\\t    slot, which is the publisher's semantics, not a shadow.\\n  1378\\t  - *In the child.* The same discipline holds transitively: a\\n  1379\\t    dependency's closure runs against the dependency's store\\n  1380\\t    instance in the child's state, its mutations are what the lift\\n  1381\\t    snapshots above, and an export a child re-exports carries that\\n  1382\\t    dependency's `:store-of`, never the child's ambient bindings.\\n  1383\\t\\n  1384\\t  **Private engine resources (r8).** r7 let `:store-get` fall back\\n  1385\\t  to the ambient store for engine-filed resource cells. That\\n  1386\\t  fallback was a forging vector: `engine/gensym` mints resource\\n  1387\\t  keys from the VM's counter (`:stream-0`, `:cursor-1`), the ids\\n  1388\\t  are predictable, and a linked module could emit\\n  1389\\t  `[:store-get :stream-0]` and read a host handle no publisher\\n  1390\\t  handed it. The split is therefore physical, not conventional:\\n  1391\\t  stream handles, cursor cells, and the FFI pair live in a private\\n  1392\\t  `:resources` table in VM state; the engine's own machinery\\n  1393\\t  (wait-set resolution, handle creation and polling, resume) reads\\n  1394\\t  and writes only that table; and no user store instruction and no\\n  1395\\t  `resolve-var` step consults it, at top level or inside a module\\n  1396\\t  closure. The ids may stay predictable -- they name nothing a\\n  1397\\t  store instruction can reach. This amends the store model of UCF\\n  1398\\t  sections 4.1 and 7.6.2 (listed with the other amendments in\\n  1399\\t  section 11, item 12): a migration slice still carries resource\\n  1400\\t  cells, remapped at resume, but beside the program store, never\\n  1401\\t  inside it.\\n  1402\\t  Executing exports inside the owning child instead was considered and\\n  1403\\t  rejected: it keeps a child VM alive for the parent's lifetime, and\\n  1404\\t  every application of an export becomes a function call across a VM\\n  1405\\t  boundary, which is the coupling the axioms forbid. Relocation costs\\n  1406\\t  one lift and one lower per install and nothing afterwards, and it is\\n  1407\\t  the same machinery UCF already needs, exercised in-process. The\\n  1408\\t  offset table it needs is one vector of `[identity offset length]` per\\n  1409\\t  positional kernel, appended to by `attach-image`; `yin.repl` already\\n  1410\\t  computes the offset and discards it, so this is recording a fact the\\n  1411\\t  loader has in hand, not a new computation. `attach-image` itself is\\n  1412\\t  the one new kernel operation this section asks for, and it is the\\n  1413\\t  append half of an operation `yin.repl` already performs.\\n  1414\\t- **`refused` restores every waiter with the refusal** as the effect's\\n  1415\\t  error, and the install entry is removed so a later require may try\\n  1416\\t  again.\\n  1417\\t\\n  1418\\tThe alternative, per-format export locators in the manifest (a body pc\\n  1419\\tfor stack images, a segment and pc for semantic ones), was considered\\n  1420\\tand rejected: it persists four descriptions of a fact that one\\n  1421\\tevaluation derives, and it would make the manifest's shape depend on\\n  1422\\twhich backends exist. This is *derive, don't persist* at the module\\n  1423\\tgrain.\\n  1424\\t\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/1790256330629-reviewer-universal-linker-consensus-r8.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log:13:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_5\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg -n ':yin.k/store|resources|resource' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '1,125p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '808,848p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"11:2. **Recursive Portable Encoding:** Section 1.1 permits host functions and local stream handles in the environment. A recursive encoding over a **disjoint tagged grammar** is required — one a program's own map literals cannot counterfeit — to encode these local references into portable descriptors, together with per-primitive **semantic profiles** (a name recovers a binding, not a meaning) and explicit, total failure modes for un-serializable resources. Cursor aliasing is observable and must survive the round trip.\\n13:4. **Ownership Arbitration:** Emitting a continuation does not transfer ownership. Exclusion requires a **grounded authority** — a resource the grantor's boundary actually possesses, named in the value, independent of carriers — plus an **exporting state** that fences the source before publication, and **effect fencing** with authority epochs and stable operation ids, because absence of a lapse record is never evidence of tenure.\\n41:plus a reason and resource ids, with no handle and no closure attached. That\\n100: :yin.k/store       {…}                        ; §7.6.2  the store slice restored as an isolated store\\n108:`:yin.k/cells`, `:yin.k/store`, `:yin.k/scheduler`), *what world*\\n467:A parked machine was waiting for something. That something is a resource of\\n469:the wait in resource-independent terms so the resumer can re-establish an\\n734:host resources behind stream descriptors and named, profiled primitives,\\n822::yin.k/store {counter 41\\n842:receiver key participates; resource entries are remapped to fresh local\\n843:keys (cursor cells per §7.5.3, stream handles per §7.4.3), and non-resource\\n976:boundary that possesses the resource** and must reclaim *by acting on what\\n985:**The possessed resource is the occurrence's execution ledger**: the set of\\n1089:admission resource, never check-then-act. An effect whose incarnation's\\n1091:`dao.lease.md`'s \\\"fencing from the resource\\\" for durable resources, and it\\n1115:from the resource.\\\" No callback tells a lapsed holder to stop; it stops at\\n1143:loss breaks exclusive custody irreparably — the ledger was the resource —\\n1147:duties*: a judge whose durability matches the resource).\\n1169:end the prior tenure). Its inventory of possessed resources is the set of\\n1187:   refusal; compute `:yin.k/requires` and `:yin.k/store` by the fixed point\\n   808\\tundeclared footprint cannot be closed — the result distinguishes\\n   809\\t`:complete` from `:incomplete` from `:blocked` (§7.6.5); a fixed point that\\n   810\\tcould not finish is reported as such, not passed off as a satisfied closure.\\n   811\\t\\n   812\\t### 7.6.2 The store slice, restored as an isolated store\\n   813\\t\\n   814\\tThe store S holds `def` results, stream handles, cursor entries, and the FFI\\n   815\\tpair (§4.1). None of it is global in the UCF sense; it is the emitter's. A\\n   816\\tcontinuation carries the **reachable slice**: every store key the fixed\\n   817\\tpoint of §7.6.1 can name — every free `:var` obligation for which the\\n   818\\temitter's store has a matching key, every `:store-get`/`:store-put` operand,\\n   819\\tevery stream/cursor cell — with values encoded by §7.5:\\n   820\\t\\n   821\\t```clojure\\n   822\\t:yin.k/store {counter 41\\n   823\\t              'log {:yin.k/tag :yin.k/stream …}}\\n   824\\t```\\n   825\\t\\n   826\\tThe FFI pair is never in the slice — a program that names the pair's store\\n   827\\tkeys directly holds handles, and the lift refuses it (§7.5.4). A resumer has\\n   828\\tits own pair; outstanding calls route per §7.4.3.\\n   829\\t\\n   830\\t**There is no merge.** The first draft said, in two places, both that the\\n   831\\tslice wins on collision and that the slice merges *under* the local store —\\n   832\\ta contradiction, and beneath it a real defect: resolution is env → store →\\n   833\\tprimitives (`resolve-var`), so a resumer whose local store happens to bind\\n   834\\t`+` would change what the migrated program resolves, without any collision\\n   835\\tat all. Merging in either order is the wrong shape. **The resumed task runs\\n   836\\tin an isolated execution store**: a fresh store whose contents are exactly\\n   837\\tthe carried slice, the remapped cells of §7.5.3, and the resumer's FFI pair\\n   838\\t— nothing else of the resumer's, nothing of any other task's. A name absent\\n   839\\tfrom the slice resolves through primitives and modules at the resumer\\n   840\\texactly as it did at the emitter, because there is no third store to\\n   841\\tintervene; a slice key cannot collide with a receiver task's key because no\\n   842\\treceiver key participates; resource entries are remapped to fresh local\\n   843\\tkeys (cursor cells per §7.5.3, stream handles per §7.4.3), and non-resource\\n   844\\tentries keep their keys verbatim. \\\"Unreachable store entries do not travel\\\"\\n   845\\tsurvives unchanged — anything the program did not observe is not the\\n   846\\tprogram's state — and nothing else shares state with the resumed task by\\n   847\\taccident.\\n   848\\t\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/1790256330629-reviewer-universal-linker-consensus-r8.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log:15:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_6\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | sed -n '1978,1995p;1908,1924p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"  1908\\t          src/cljc/yin/vm/content.cljc (retire load-rows,\\n  1909\\t                                        fetch-vector)              (M2)\\n  1910\\t          src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc, semantic.cljc,\\n  1911\\t          debruijn/stack.cljc, debruijn/register.cljc\\n  1912\\t            (attach-image; offset table and image\\n  1913\\t             identities on the positional kernels; row index\\n  1914\\t             on the walker; closures carry :store-of; the\\n  1915\\t             register frame drops :segment and :hash; store\\n  1916\\t             routing and its frame threading)                      (M4)\\n  1917\\t          src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc  (append-*-image over attach-image;\\n  1918\\t                                   the four streams)          (M4, M5)\\n  1919\\t          docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md\\n  1920\\t            (section 7.4.1 rows, 7.4.3 variants, 7.5.1 marker\\n  1921\\t             keys :yin.k/binding and :yin.k/store-of)              (M4)\\n  1922\\tDepends:  dao.jing (segment-key, segment-matches?, materialize!)\\n  1923\\t          dao.jing.remote, dao.jing.remote.step, dao.stream.rpc\\n  1924\\t          yin.vm (validate-rows, free-names, primitive-profiles)\\n  1978\\t11. The scheduler registry holds no function that arrived by linking\\n  1979\\t    and no lowered bindings, only the portable slice; every task-local\\n  1980\\t    `:bindings` was lowered from a slice an isolated child evaluation\\n  1981\\t    produced, which reached halt with every export bound.\\n  1982\\t12. UCF section 7.4.1 carries `:link-request`, `:link-response`, and\\n  1983\\t    `:install` safepoints, section 7.4.3 their pending variants, section\\n  1984\\t    7.5.1 the `:yin.k/binding` closure variants and `:yin.k/store-of`\\n  1985\\t    key of section 7.3, sections 4.1 and 7.6.2 the private\\n  1986\\t    engine-resource table of section 7.3, and a parked entry of each\\n  1987\\t    kind lifts and lowers through UCF's round-trip test.\\n  1988\\t13. Every swapped-image and swapped-name case in M4 refuses before load.\\n  1989\\t14. The assertion policy of section 8.2 refuses `:ambiguous-name`,\\n  1990\\t    discards unproven assertions as `:unauthenticated`, and ignores\\n  1991\\t    undeclared principals, at a fixed snapshot.\\n  1992\\t15. A module that defines a name and then reads it in the main\\n  1993\\t    sequence produces no obligation for that name; a main-sequence\\n  1994\\t    read before the definition is `:use-before-definition`; a read\\n  1995\\t    inside a body is free of obligation only when an unconditional\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/1790256330629-reviewer-universal-linker-consensus-r8.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log:18:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_8\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | sed -n '1085,1215p;1310,1410p;1910,1930p;1975,1992p;2120,2175p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"  1085\\t  segment and no image identity; a semantic closure carries a local\\n  1086\\t  segment id minted by the child's loader. Published as they are, those\\n  1087\\t  coordinates are meaningless in the parent, so bindings cross the\\n  1088\\t  child-to-parent boundary the way a continuation crosses any boundary:\\n  1089\\t  by lift and lower. The `linked` transition is four acts: acts 1\\n  1090\\t  and 2 run once, at `validated`; acts 3 and 4 run once per\\n  1091\\t  receiving task -- the parent at `linked`, and each waiter or later\\n  1092\\t  requirer at its own restore.\\n  1093\\t  1. *The child's export slice is lifted* to UCF's portable encoding\\n  1094\\t     (section 7.5.1, amended below): each closure becomes a\\n  1095\\t     `:yin.k/closure` marker naming its **origin image** and its entry\\n  1096\\t     relative to that image, with its captured values encoded\\n  1097\\t     recursively; every other value encodes by the same grammar. The\\n  1098\\t     origin is not assumed to be the module's own image: a closure the\\n  1099\\t     child obtained from a dependency and re-exported originates in\\n  1100\\t     that dependency's image, and the lift records whichever image the\\n  1101\\t     closure's coordinates fall in. The lift is refused as\\n  1102\\t     `:yin.k/non-portable` for the same leaves UCF refuses (a host\\n  1103\\t     object, an in-memory handle), and a refused lift refuses the\\n  1104\\t     install: an export that cannot cross a boundary is not an export.\\n  1105\\t  2. *The origin set is checked.* The lifted slice's\\n  1106\\t     `:yin.k/requires :yin.k/segments` enumerates every origin image it\\n  1107\\t     names, transitively through captured values. Each must be an image\\n  1108\\t     this scheduler verified: the module's own, or one delivered for an\\n  1109\\t     install in this scheduler's history (held under `:installs` or the\\n  1110\\t     registry by identity). An origin identity the scheduler never\\n  1111\\t     verified is `:foreign-image`, and the install is refused; a\\n  1112\\t     verified image is never re-fetched for this step.\\n  1113\\t  3. *The receiving task attaches every origin image its kernel does\\n  1114\\t     not yet hold*, non-destructively. The ordinary loaders\\n  1115\\t     (`vm-load-rows`, `load-vector`, `load-image`) are entry points\\n  1116\\t     for a fresh run: they set the program counter, control, or root,\\n  1117\\t     and reset the operand stack and frames. A receiving task at its\\n  1118\\t     restore is a parked task whose registers must survive, so each\\n  1119\\t     kernel exposes a second\\n  1120\\t     operation, **`attach-image`**, that extends the kernel's code\\n  1121\\t     space and touches nothing else. Its contract, per kernel:\\n  1122\\t     - *semantic*: the verified vector is added under a fresh local id\\n  1123\\t       and the address is written to the alias column; `:control`,\\n  1124\\t       `:env`, `:stack`, `:k`, every `:return` frame's segment and pc,\\n  1125\\t       and the store are unchanged, because existing local ids are\\n  1126\\t       never renumbered (UCF section 7.3.4, \\\"local-id immutability\\n  1127\\t       stands\\\");\\n  1128\\t     - *stack* and *register*: the image is relocated by the current\\n  1129\\t       held length and appended to `:segment` (and, for the register\\n  1130\\t       VM, `:bodies` shifted likewise), and one `[identity offset\\n  1131\\t       length]` row is appended to the **offset table**, a kernel-state\\n  1132\\t       vector the lift of act 1 also reads, whose first row is the\\n  1133\\t       base image `[H 0 n]` recorded by `load-image`, so the table is\\n  1134\\t       the kernel's complete image inventory; `:pc`, `:stack`,\\n  1135\\t       `:frames`, `:continuation` (every `:return-pc` and `:frames`\\n  1136\\t       in it), and `:registers` are unchanged, because appending never\\n  1137\\t       moves an instruction already held; `:hash` becomes the identity\\n  1138\\t       of the new concatenation, as `yin.repl`'s `append-*-image`\\n  1139\\t       computes it, and is from r6 on **never a restore key**: a\\n  1140\\t       concatenation identity changes at every attach, so an entry or\\n  1141\\t       continuation that recorded it would be refused by the first\\n  1142\\t       later attach;\\n  1143\\t     - *walker*: the verified tree's rows are added to the kernel's\\n  1144\\t       row set (`:code-segment` expansion) keyed by their ids; the\\n  1145\\t       current node, environment, and continuation are unchanged.\\n  1146\\t     `yin.repl`'s `append-stack-image` and `append-register-image`\\n  1147\\t     already do the relocation and append but then set `:pc` to the\\n  1148\\t     new offset to start the next input; `attach-image` is that\\n  1149\\t     function without the final `:pc` assignment, and `yin.repl` is\\n  1150\\t     rewritten to call `attach-image` and then set `:pc` itself.\\n  1151\\t\\n  1152\\t     **Hash-safe restore (r6).** A parked or waiting entry records,\\n  1153\\t     beside `:format`, the **image identity** its positions are\\n  1154\\t     relative to -- the offset-table row its `:pc` falls in -- and\\n  1155\\t     restores against the table, never against `:hash`.\\n  1156\\t     `stack-restore` and `register-restore` today refuse any entry\\n  1157\\t     whose `:hash` differs from the loaded image's and then write\\n  1158\\t     `:segment` back from the entry, so an attach mid-park would make\\n  1159\\t     every parked entry unrestorable or rewind the kernel to the\\n  1160\\t     pre-attach concatenation, discarding images other entries and\\n  1161\\t     closures still name (the semantic entry needs no change: its\\n  1162\\t     `:segment` is a local id that attach never renumbers). From r6\\n  1163\\t     the identity check is table membership -- the row exists, the\\n  1164\\t     table only grows, and an absent row is a host assembly defect --\\n  1165\\t     and the entry restores registers and never assigns `:segment`:\\n  1166\\t     the code space is kernel state, and since appending moves\\n  1167\\t     nothing, the entry's absolute pcs and stack bases still name\\n  1168\\t     what they named. A lifted continuation (act 1) carries its pcs\\n  1169\\t     image-relatively, as `[identity rel-pc]` pairs for `:pc`, every\\n  1170\\t     `:return-pc`, and every body reference, so a lower into another\\n  1171\\t     kernel rebases each by that kernel's table row; this is the\\n  1172\\t     rebasing r5 lacked, and same-kernel restore needs none of it.\\n  1173\\t     An image already present is not attached twice; the alias column\\n  1174\\t     or the offset table says so.\\n  1175\\t\\n  1176\\t     **Grow-only returns (r7).** Parked restores are one restore path;\\n  1177\\t     ordinary function returns are the other, and r6 left them alone.\\n  1178\\t     The register VM's call frame today carries the caller's whole\\n  1179\\t     `:segment` and `:hash` (its `:call` builds the frame), and its\\n  1180\\t     return transition writes both back into the VM\\n  1181\\t     (`return-transition`): an image a nested require attached while\\n  1182\\t     the call was in flight is discarded the moment the callee\\n  1183\\t     returns, though the grown code space is exactly what the\\n  1184\\t     resumed caller must run in. From r7 one rule covers every\\n  1185\\t     transition: **no frame, entry, or restore assigns a code-space\\n  1186\\t     value; `:segment`, `:hash`, and the offset table are kernel\\n  1187\\t     state that only the loaders and `attach-image` write.** The\\n  1188\\t     register frame drops `:segment` and `:hash` -- its absolute\\n  1189\\t     `:return-pc` survives every later append -- and its return\\n  1190\\t     restores pc, frames, registers, and continuation only. The\\n  1191\\t     stack frame already conforms (`:return-pc`, `:frames`,\\n  1192\\t     `:stack-base`; its `:return` never touches `:segment`), and the\\n  1193\\t     semantic frame's `:segment` is a local id that attach never\\n  1194\\t     renumbers. A lift that needs a frame pc's image derives the row\\n  1195\\t     from the offset table at lift time, so frames carry no identity\\n  1196\\t     either.\\n  1197\\t  4. *Each receiving task lowers the encoding* against its own\\n  1198\\t     coordinate space by the mapping below and writes the result into\\n  1199\\t     its own `:modules` entry as `:bindings`, which is what\\n  1200\\t     `resolve-var`'s module step reads. The scheduler's registry\\n  1201\\t     value never holds lowered bindings: it gains `{'foo {:manifest m\\n  1202\\t     :address a :derivation d :slice lifted :stores {addr store}}}`,\\n  1203\\t     the portable encoding of acts 1 to 3. Revision r5 lowered once\\n  1204\\t     into one parent's coordinates and published the result to every\\n  1205\\t     waiter; two tasks holding images of different lengths attach\\n  1206\\t     the same origin at different offsets and mint different local\\n  1207\\t     ids, so one task's lowered slice would misplace every body pc\\n  1208\\t     and alias in the other's. Every waiter is restored with `'foo`\\n  1209\\t     as its value after its own attach and lower; so is every later\\n  1210\\t     requirer.\\n  1211\\t\\n  1212\\t  **The closure marker, and its lift and lower per binding discipline.**\\n  1213\\t  UCF section 7.5.1 defines the closure marker for the named semantic\\n  1214\\t  VM. The de Bruijn kernels are positional and nameless, so the marker\\n  1215\\t  gains a `:yin.k/binding` key with two variants, and a module's\\n  1310\\t  `encode` recurse through frames and environments, so a captured\\n  1311\\t  closure is relocated by the same rules and its origin joins the\\n  1312\\t  origin set of act 2.\\n  1313\\t\\n  1314\\t  **The module store (r6, amended r7).** A child runs with an\\n  1315\\t  isolated store, and its exports must not leak that isolation. A\\n  1316\\t  module that binds `(def x 1)` and exports `(fn [] x)` publishes a\\n  1317\\t  closure whose free read of `x` would, if only the closure value\\n  1318\\t  crossed the boundary, resolve through the receiving task's store\\n  1319\\t  and fall through to its primitives and module registry -- where\\n  1320\\t  `x` is unbound or bound to something the publisher never saw. The\\n  1321\\t  stores therefore cross with the exports, under UCF section 7.6.2's\\n  1322\\t  slice discipline:\\n  1323\\t  - *Lift.* One snapshot is encoded per module, not just the child's\\n  1324\\t    own: the child's halted store under its manifest address, and\\n  1325\\t    for every dependency whose store the exports reach transitively\\n  1326\\t    (a re-exported closure carries that dependency's `:store-of`),\\n  1327\\t    that dependency's store instance as it stands in the child's\\n  1328\\t    state at halt -- including mutations the child made through the\\n  1329\\t    dependency's own closures, which a snapshot of the child's store\\n  1330\\t    alone would not carry. A `:store-of` with no snapshot is a lift\\n  1331\\t    refusal, `:yin.k/non-portable` with\\n  1332\\t    `:yin.k/kind :missing-module-store`. Each snapshot encodes as a\\n  1333\\t    `:yin.k/store` slice (UCF 7.6.2); whatever that grammar refuses\\n  1334\\t    (a host object in the store; engine resources cannot be, by the\\n  1335\\t    private-resource rule below) refuses the lift and the install.\\n  1336\\t  - *Marker.* Every closure marker names the store its body resolves\\n  1337\\t    against, `:yin.k/store-of m` with `m` the module's manifest\\n  1338\\t    address -- an amendment to UCF section 7.5.1 listed in section\\n  1339\\t    11, item 12. The snapshots travel once per slice, not per\\n  1340\\t    closure.\\n  1341\\t  - *Lower.* Each receiving task instantiates every snapshot into\\n  1342\\t    `:module-stores {m {sym value}}` in its own state, one instance\\n  1343\\t    per module: a store the task already holds stands as\\n  1344\\t    instantiated -- first link wins, and a later slice never\\n  1345\\t    overwrites a live instance -- while snapshots it lacks are\\n  1346\\t    instantiated fresh, so a task sees exactly one store per module.\\n  1347\\t    Nothing is written into any task's ambient store: this is\\n  1348\\t    7.6.2's \\\"there is no merge\\\" rule at the module grain -- a\\n  1349\\t    receiver binding of the same name can neither supply nor shadow\\n  1350\\t    the module's value.\\n  1351\\t  - *Run time.* While a closure carrying `:store-of m` executes, the\\n  1352\\t    module store of m is its **active store**: free reads resolve\\n  1353\\t    through it first and the receiver's free env, primitives, and\\n  1354\\t    registry second; the ambient store does not participate. Direct\\n  1355\\t    store instructions route by the same context -- `:store-put`\\n  1356\\t    (and the `:vm/store-put` effect) writes the active module\\n  1357\\t    store, and `:store-get` reads it with **no ambient fallback**:\\n  1358\\t    a key the module store lacks is absent, exactly as a top-level\\n  1359\\t    read of an absent key is; engine resources are unreachable\\n  1360\\t    either way, by the private-resource rule below. The context\\n  1361\\t    threads through calls: a frame records the caller's active\\n  1362\\t    `:store-of` (or none), applying a closure makes that closure's\\n  1363\\t    `:store-of` active, and returning restores the frame's, so a\\n  1364\\t    module closure calling another module's closure routes each\\n  1365\\t    body's reads and writes to its own module. A tail call that\\n  1366\\t    empties the continuation -- the closure's body was the whole\\n  1367\\t    computation -- clears the active context with the last frame,\\n  1368\\t    and the VM admits new top-level input only with no active\\n  1369\\t    context, which `yin.repl`'s append asserts, so input after a\\n  1370\\t    module closure applied never routes through a stale module\\n  1371\\t    store. Sibling exports in one task share one store instance,\\n  1372\\t    so a write one makes is visible to the next; each task holds\\n  1373\\t    its own instances, so writes never cross tasks. A declared\\n  1374\\t    obligation the store does not hold falls through to the\\n  1375\\t    receiver's bindings exactly as step 5b checked them; a\\n  1376\\t    conditional definition that ran overwrites the module's own\\n  1377\\t    slot, which is the publisher's semantics, not a shadow.\\n  1378\\t  - *In the child.* The same discipline holds transitively: a\\n  1379\\t    dependency's closure runs against the dependency's store\\n  1380\\t    instance in the child's state, its mutations are what the lift\\n  1381\\t    snapshots above, and an export a child re-exports carries that\\n  1382\\t    dependency's `:store-of`, never the child's ambient bindings.\\n  1383\\t\\n  1384\\t  **Private engine resources (r8).** r7 let `:store-get` fall back\\n  1385\\t  to the ambient store for engine-filed resource cells. That\\n  1386\\t  fallback was a forging vector: `engine/gensym` mints resource\\n  1387\\t  keys from the VM's counter (`:stream-0`, `:cursor-1`), the ids\\n  1388\\t  are predictable, and a linked module could emit\\n  1389\\t  `[:store-get :stream-0]` and read a host handle no publisher\\n  1390\\t  handed it. The split is therefore physical, not conventional:\\n  1391\\t  stream handles, cursor cells, and the FFI pair live in a private\\n  1392\\t  `:resources` table in VM state; the engine's own machinery\\n  1393\\t  (wait-set resolution, handle creation and polling, resume) reads\\n  1394\\t  and writes only that table; and no user store instruction and no\\n  1395\\t  `resolve-var` step consults it, at top level or inside a module\\n  1396\\t  closure. The ids may stay predictable -- they name nothing a\\n  1397\\t  store instruction can reach. This amends the store model of UCF\\n  1398\\t  sections 4.1 and 7.6.2 (listed with the other amendments in\\n  1399\\t  section 11, item 12): a migration slice still carries resource\\n  1400\\t  cells, remapped at resume, but beside the program store, never\\n  1401\\t  inside it.\\n  1402\\t  Executing exports inside the owning child instead was considered and\\n  1403\\t  rejected: it keeps a child VM alive for the parent's lifetime, and\\n  1404\\t  every application of an export becomes a function call across a VM\\n  1405\\t  boundary, which is the coupling the axioms forbid. Relocation costs\\n  1406\\t  one lift and one lower per install and nothing afterwards, and it is\\n  1407\\t  the same machinery UCF already needs, exercised in-process. The\\n  1408\\t  offset table it needs is one vector of `[identity offset length]` per\\n  1409\\t  positional kernel, appended to by `attach-image`; `yin.repl` already\\n  1410\\t  computes the offset and discards it, so this is recording a fact the\\n  1910\\t          src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc, semantic.cljc,\\n  1911\\t          debruijn/stack.cljc, debruijn/register.cljc\\n  1912\\t            (attach-image; offset table and image\\n  1913\\t             identities on the positional kernels; row index\\n  1914\\t             on the walker; closures carry :store-of; the\\n  1915\\t             register frame drops :segment and :hash; store\\n  1916\\t             routing and its frame threading)                      (M4)\\n  1917\\t          src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc  (append-*-image over attach-image;\\n  1918\\t                                   the four streams)          (M4, M5)\\n  1919\\t          docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md\\n  1920\\t            (section 7.4.1 rows, 7.4.3 variants, 7.5.1 marker\\n  1921\\t             keys :yin.k/binding and :yin.k/store-of)              (M4)\\n  1922\\tDepends:  dao.jing (segment-key, segment-matches?, materialize!)\\n  1923\\t          dao.jing.remote, dao.jing.remote.step, dao.stream.rpc\\n  1924\\t          yin.vm (validate-rows, free-names, primitive-profiles)\\n  1925\\t          yin.vm.code (well-formed-vector?)\\n  1926\\t          yin.vm.ledger (derive-record, verify-derivation)\\n  1927\\t          yin.vm.debruijn-code, yin.vm.debruijn-register-code\\n  1928\\tMust not change:\\n  1929\\t          image-hash, register-hash, segment-key, the opcode\\n  1930\\t          tables, dao.stream, dao.jing. No kernel's step function\\n  1975\\t10. `(require 'foo)` on each of the four backends, over ring buffers,\\n  1976\\t    parks, links, installs through the child phases, and resumes with\\n  1977\\t    `'foo`; the same over the JVM WebSocket content path.\\n  1978\\t11. The scheduler registry holds no function that arrived by linking\\n  1979\\t    and no lowered bindings, only the portable slice; every task-local\\n  1980\\t    `:bindings` was lowered from a slice an isolated child evaluation\\n  1981\\t    produced, which reached halt with every export bound.\\n  1982\\t12. UCF section 7.4.1 carries `:link-request`, `:link-response`, and\\n  1983\\t    `:install` safepoints, section 7.4.3 their pending variants, section\\n  1984\\t    7.5.1 the `:yin.k/binding` closure variants and `:yin.k/store-of`\\n  1985\\t    key of section 7.3, sections 4.1 and 7.6.2 the private\\n  1986\\t    engine-resource table of section 7.3, and a parked entry of each\\n  1987\\t    kind lifts and lowers through UCF's round-trip test.\\n  1988\\t13. Every swapped-image and swapped-name case in M4 refuses before load.\\n  1989\\t14. The assertion policy of section 8.2 refuses `:ambiguous-name`,\\n  1990\\t    discards unproven assertions as `:unauthenticated`, and ignores\\n  1991\\t    undeclared principals, at a fixed snapshot.\\n  1992\\t15. A module that defines a name and then reads it in the main\\n  2120\\t  The stepped core follows that rule once OD-1 is accepted; until then\\n  2121\\t  it classifies unknown content-server answers as `transport-error`, as\\n  2122\\t  `dao.stream.observe/step` does.\\n  2123\\t- **Cross-task module stores.** Each receiving task lowers its own\\n  2124\\t  module-store instance (section 7.3), so writes never cross tasks. A\\n  2125\\t  composition wanting one shared, mutable module instance across a\\n  2126\\t  scheduler's tasks needs a scheduler-owned cell and an effect\\n  2127\\t  discipline for writing it; that is not this document's mechanism.\\n  2128\\t\\n  2129\\t## 13. Reconciliation record\\n  2130\\t\\n  2131\\t### 13.1 Revision r8 (round 7, `gpt-6-sol`)\\n  2132\\t\\n  2133\\tThe round-7 review closed the grow-only-returns item (no action)\\n  2134\\tand left two partial findings. The round-7 findings file was not\\n  2135\\tpresent in `collab/`; r8 worked from the two items in the task.\\n  2136\\tEach, and what r8 did:\\n  2137\\t\\n  2138\\t| Item                              | Resolution                        |\\n  2139\\t|-----------------------------------|-----------------------------------|\\n  2140\\t| r7's `:store-get` fallback to the | Agreed; r7 rule withdrawn. Engine |\\n  2141\\t| ambient store was a forging       | resources (stream handles, cursor |\\n  2142\\t| vector: resource keys are         | cells, the FFI pair) move to a    |\\n  2143\\t| counter-minted and predictable,   | private `:resources` table no     |\\n  2144\\t| so a module could read a host     | store instruction or `resolve-var`|\\n  2145\\t| handle it was never handed; the   | step reads; store instructions    |\\n  2146\\t| active context could go stale     | have no ambient fallback at all;  |\\n  2147\\t| after a tail call                 | ids may stay predictable, they    |\\n  2148\\t|                                   | name nothing reachable (a UCF    |\\n  2149\\t|                                   | 4.1/7.6.2 amendment, criterion    |\\n  2150\\t|                                   | 12). A tail call that empties the |\\n  2151\\t|                                   | continuation clears the active    |\\n  2152\\t|                                   | context, and new top-level input  |\\n  2153\\t|                                   | is admitted only with none active |\\n  2154\\t|                                   | (7.3 private engine resources,    |\\n  2155\\t|                                   | run-time rule; criterion 23; M4   |\\n  2156\\t|                                   | forged-key and stale-context      |\\n  2157\\t|                                   | tests).                           |\\n  2158\\t| r7 conflated decode time with     | Agreed. Two stages: the row       |\\n  2159\\t| evaluation time for the walker's  | decoder annotates each decoded    |\\n  2160\\t| lambda row id; the closure is     | `:lambda` node with its source    |\\n  2161\\t| instantiated only when the node   | row id (or a side index; one node |\\n  2162\\t| is evaluated                      | per row id, so unambiguous), and  |\\n  2163\\t|                                   | the runtime `:lambda` transition  |\\n  2164\\t|                                   | copies the annotation into the    |\\n  2165\\t|                                   | closure it instantiates; the lift |\\n  2166\\t|                                   | verifies body slot and params as  |\\n  2167\\t|                                   | before (7.3 walker rule,          |\\n  2168\\t|                                   | criterion 23).                    |\\n  2169\\t\\n  2170\\t### 13.2 Revision r7 (round 6, `gpt-6-sol`)\\n  2171\\t\\n  2172\\tThe round-6 review closed the dominance and scanner item (no\\n  2173\\taction), left two partial findings, and added one relocation edge.\\n  2174\\tEach, and what r7 did:\\n  2175\\t\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/1790256330629-reviewer-universal-linker-consensus-r8.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log:20:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_9\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg -n ':yin.k/stream|resources|7\\\\\\\\.5\\\\\\\\.4|7\\\\\\\\.6\\\\\\\\.2|stream-ref|in-memory handle|:yin.k/requires|:yin.k/cells' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:1058:  section 7.6.2 applied to loading), its own wait-set, and a module\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:1103:     object, an in-memory handle), and a refused lift refuses the\\ndocs/design/yin.